{
  "dom_id": "step-e9356",
  "name": "Example: Identifying an Equation That Generates a Slope Field Using Stationary Points",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/4adbd41200bb3fdcd75f7f19530ca810cb67970b732e897845bfd621fee180b3.png)\n\n \n\nThe differential equation that generates the slope field shown above is given by $y' = (x-a)(y-b).$ Find the value of $a + b.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14045300/assets/5401c18589eeafd8ac4139de98fb41879d03c0731938c42b2dae50da629de0ce.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x = -2$ and $y = 2,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nSo, our differential equation must be\n\n$$\ny' = (x+2)(y-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $a = -2,$ $b = 2,$ and $a + b=0.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
